#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: rozum-meetings
version: 0.1.0
description: The rozum meeting transcript — one Tk source for the UCC web client and the terminal one.
frontend: tui
---
# rozum meetings

Stage A of `docs/specs/ucc-meetings-in-tk.md`: the meeting transcript from ONE source, emitted as
both the React client in the control centre and the native ratatui client that is meant to replace
the hand-written `rozum meetings attach`.

**Why this looks so plain.** It is a table, and it is a table on purpose. The terminal target
registers a managed GET only through a `DataTable(Remote …)`, a `SignalText` or a `ModelView` — a
`View.For` does not record its fetch signal, and `forJsonView` (per-row custom rendering) is a web
primitive. So date dividers, per-row severity colours and a bold author are reachable on React and
not on the terminal. Since the whole point is one source with no target check, this view lives in
the INTERSECTION of the two targets. Anything richer has to arrive as an emitter capability first.

**Why `badge` and `time` are plain columns.** They are computed by the daemon
(`StoredTurn::badge()` / `time_hm()`) and shipped in the read API rather than derived here. A
generated client cannot format a unix epoch or re-implement the incident-badge rules, and if it
tried, there would be two implementations to drift apart — which is exactly what generating the
client is supposed to end.

**Auth is configuration, not a target branch.** `ROZUM_MEETING_TOKEN` is an issued operator token
(`rozum meetings token issue <handle> --role observer`), sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`. The
daemon grew Bearer support for exactly this client: Basic needs base64 of `":" + token` and a view
has no base64, which previously forced this source to accept a pre-built header instead of a token.

**Two caveats, both real, neither a defect of this file.**

1. The terminal target silently drops the header signal today (`ureq::get(url).call()`, no headers)
   — filed upstream as `tui-fetch-headers` — so the emitted terminal binary reads an
   unauthenticated fixture, not the live daemon.
2. ⚠️ **Do not set `ROZUM_MEETING_TOKEN` when emitting the terminal target.** `ssc run --v1`
   executes this program in the emitting process, so `env(...)` is read at BUILD time and anything
   derived from it is folded into the generated Rust as a literal — a token set here would be
   compiled into the binary. Today the header is dropped so nothing leaks; the moment headers are
   honoured naively, it would. The durable fix is a credential the target's RUNTIME resolves,
   proposed upstream as `ui-fetch-credentials`. Until then: emit without the token, supply it to the
   running client.

[env](std/os.ssc)
[signal, intervalTick, fetchUrlSignal, fetchUrlSignalTo, fetchActionClear, incSignal, fieldColumn, rowLinkAction](std/ui/primitives.ssc)
[lower](std/ui/lower.ssc)
[defaultTheme](std/ui/theme.ssc)
[vstack, hstack](std/ui/layout.ssc)
[heading, text](std/ui/typography.ssc)
[actionButton, textField](std/ui/input.ssc)
[remoteTable](std/ui/data.ssc)

```scalascript
val apiBase = env("ROZUM_MEETING_BASE").getOrElse("http://127.0.0.1:8401")
val room    = env("ROZUM_MEETING_ROOM").getOrElse("rozum")
val day     = env("ROZUM_MEETING_DATE").getOrElse("today")
val token   = env("ROZUM_MEETING_TOKEN").getOrElse("")

// ONE tick drives everything that re-reads: the clock advances it every 3 s, the refresh button
// bumps it, and a successful post bumps it. A meeting client that only updated on a keypress would
// not be a replacement for `rozum meetings attach`, which shows a message the moment it lands.
// The terminal loop polls with a 100 ms timeout, so 3 s is a floor rather than a guarantee.
val refresh = intervalTick("meetingsRefresh", 3000)
val headers = signal("meetingsHeaders", "{\"Authorization\":\"Bearer " + token + "\"}")

// The room picker writes a READY-MADE url into `here`, and the transcript follows it. The client
// never composes an address — it cannot, there is no string composition on the terminal target —
// so `/rooms` ships the finished url per row, exactly as it ships `badge` and `time`.
val here = signal("meetingsUrl", apiBase + "/rooms/" + room + "/messages/" + day)

val transcript = fetchUrlSignalTo("meetingsTranscript", here, refresh, headers)

val roomList = fetchUrlSignal("meetingsRooms", apiBase + "/rooms", refresh, headers)

// `mentions` is what this daemon can honestly count as unread: messages ADDRESSING you that you
// have not seen. A raw unread number would need a per-viewer read marker that does not exist.
val roomTable = remoteTable(
  roomList,
  [
    fieldColumn("Room", "name"),
    fieldColumn("Last", "last"),
    fieldColumn("@you", "mentions")
  ],
  "entries",
  [rowLinkAction("open", here, "url")],
  "name"
)

// rowsPath "messages" drills the daemon's envelope {room,date,from,count,…,messages:[…]};
// the web drills it and the tui parses it with the same string. rowKeyPath is `ts` — a stable
// integral field that is actually present on every row (`id` is derived Rust-side and not sent).
val transcriptTable = remoteTable(
  transcript,
  [
    fieldColumn("Time", "time"),
    fieldColumn("Who", "display_name"),
    fieldColumn("", "badge"),
    fieldColumn("Message", "content")
  ],
  "messages",
  [],
  "ts"
)

// The composer. `draft` is what the user types; `send` posts it and, on success, bumps `refresh` —
// which is the transcript's fetch tick, so the list re-reads with no extra wiring.
//
// The body is sent VERBATIM: a terminal target cannot compose strings (there is no computedSignal
// in the static model), so it cannot wrap the text in JSON. The daemon accepts the plain-text form
// for exactly this reason — same call as `badge` and `time`, put the work where it can be done once.
val draft = signal("meetingsDraft", "")
val send = fetchActionClear("POST", apiBase + "/rooms/" + room + "/messages", draft, refresh, headers)

// Day paging is the SAME interaction as room switching — pick a row, the transcript follows —
// rather than a bespoke PgUp, which would need date arithmetic the terminal target cannot do.
// Newest first, because a reader opening the list wants today.
val dayList = fetchUrlSignal("meetingsDays", apiBase + "/rooms/" + room + "/days", refresh, headers)

val dayTable = remoteTable(
  dayList,
  [fieldColumn("Day", "date"), fieldColumn("#", "count")],
  "entries",
  [rowLinkAction("open", here, "url")],
  "date"
)

val sidebar = vstack(gap = 1)(
  heading(1, "rozum"),
  roomTable,
  dayTable,
  textField(draft, "message"),
  actionButton(send, "send"),
  actionButton(incSignal(refresh), "refresh")
)

def view() = lower(
  hstack(gap = 2)(sidebar, transcriptTable),
  defaultTheme
)
```
